---
title: Writing pages
order: 20
tags: [markup, authoring]
---

A page is a plain text file with an optional front matter block followed by
markup. The lexer is line-oriented; block structure comes from the way lines
begin.

## Blocks

Headings use from one to six hash marks:

```
# Document title
## Section
### Subsection
```

Paragraphs are runs of text lines separated from each other by blank lines.
Lists use `-` or `*` for unordered items and `1.` for ordered items; nesting
is done with two spaces of indentation:

```
- alpha
- beta
  - beta one
  - beta two
1. first
2. second
```

Block quotes start each line with `>` and can contain several lines. Code
blocks are fenced with three backticks, optionally with a language name:

````
```python
def greet(name):
    return f"Hello, {name}"
```
````

A thematic break is a standalone `---` line. Admonitions use a bracketed
kind tag — `[note]`, `[tip]`, `[warn]`, `[danger]`, `[info]` — followed by
the text of the callout:

```
[warn] Back up your sources before upgrading quaydoc.
```

Fragments of another page can be pulled in verbatim:

```
{% include "guide/writing.qd" %}
```

## Inline markup

Inline elements live inside paragraphs: `**bold**`, `` `code` ``,
`[[target]]` internal references, `[[target|label]]` references with a
custom label, `[label](url)` external links, `![alt](src)` images, and
backslash escapes for literal markup characters.

Here is bold **text**, a `code span`, a reference to the
[[Building a site]] page, and an external link to the
[Python documentation](https://docs.python.org/3/).

## Front matter

The optional front matter block starts the file:

```
---
title: Writing pages
order: 20
description: The markup reference.
date: 2025-01-20
tags: [markup, authoring]
draft: false
---
```

`title` is the page heading in the sidebar and the `<h1>` of the rendered
page. `order` sorts pages in the navigation and in listings. `draft: true`
excludes the page from builds. `date` feeds the Atom feed and sitemap.

[note] Unknown front matter keys are kept available to templates and are
reported as warnings by the checker.

## Escaping

A backslash before any markup character writes that character literally:
`\[` renders as `[`. Inside code spans and code blocks, no markup is
interpreted at all.
